[% META title = 'Database Configuration' %]

<!-- Documentation page for DB_CONFIG_README -->
<div class="documentation-content">




<!-- Documentation CSS is now included in the theme system -->

<div class="markdown-content">


<h2>Quick Setup</h2>



<ol><li>Place <code>db_config.json</code> in the Comserv application root directory</li></ol>

<ol><li>OR set environment variable: <code>export COMSERV_CONFIG_PATH=/path/to/config/dir</code></li></ol>



<h2>Configuration File Location</h2>



The system will look for <code>db_config.json</code> in:

<ol><li>Directory specified by <code>COMSERV_CONFIG_PATH</code> environment variable (if set)</li></ol>

<ol><li>Comserv application root directory (default fallback)</li></ol>



<h2>Example Configuration</h2>



<code></code>`json

{

  "shanta_ency": {

    "database": "ency_db_name",

    "host": "db_host",

    "port": 3306,

    "username": "db_user",

    "password": "<your-database-password>"

  },

  "shanta_forager": {

    "database": "forager_db_name",

    "host": "db_host",

    "port": 3306,

    "username": "db_user",

    "password": "<your-database-password>"

  }

}

<code></code>`



<h2>Important Notes</h2>



<ul><li>NEVER use hardcoded absolute paths to <code>db_config.json</code></li></ul>

<ul><li><code>db_config.json</code> is a <strong>tracked, credential-free template</strong>. A copy is populated with the active primary Ency credentials at deploy (or via the Docker first-run setup prompt); once K8s secrets supply them, the inline credentials are removed and the tracked file stays blank-template only. It is gitignored at runtime; the template form is committed so a fresh server has a starting point.</li></ul>

<ul><li>Live credentials must NOT remain inline in any committed/tracked file. <code>db_config.json.backup.*</code> are tracked <em>as templates</em> — keep them credential-free. Change a live password in the app at <code>/remotedb/change_password/&lt;slot&gt;</code> (admin). That form updates <code>db_config.json</code> and <code>~/.comserv/secrets/dbi/*.json</code> and will not write timestamped backup dumps.</li></ul>

<ul><li>Keep database credentials secure</li></ul>

<h2>Role-Based Runtime User (2026-08-28)</h2>
<p>The active Ency connection is chosen by <strong>priority</strong> from the
<code>~/.comserv/secrets/dbi/*.json</code> files (K8s-Secrets pattern, loaded first by
<code>Comserv::Util::RemoteDB</code>). The canonical <code>db_production_mysql.json</code> now declares three
prioritized entries for the <code>ency</code> database:</p>
<ul>
  <li><code>comserv_app</code> (priority 1) — limited DML, the preferred normal runtime user. This is the only
      DB user that should live in the deployable secret files.</li>
  <li><code>comserv_admin</code> (priority 2) — full DDL rights, used only by SchemaComparison for
      CREATE/DROP/ALTER. Store full-access users DB-backed, never in git.</li>
  <li><code>shanta_forager</code> (priority 3) — safe fallback backup; used automatically if the higher-priority
      users fail to authenticate.</li>
</ul>
<p><code>DBEncy</code> logs an <code>error</code>-level warning (surfaces as a GUI alert) when
<code>comserv_app</code> is not the active user. Admins switch the active user from the DB-user dropdown on
<code>/admin</code> (Database Management card); the current user is shown in the page footer. See
<code>database_configuration.tt</code> for the full table and the MariaDB <code>%</code> host requirement that
gates the switch to <code>comserv_app</code>.</p>

<ul><li>Do not commit <code>db_config.json</code> to version control</li></ul>

<ul><li>Ensure proper file permissions</li></ul>



<h2>Running the Application</h2>



Use the provided script to automatically set up the environment:

<code></code>`

cd /path/to/Comserv

./run_app.sh

<code></code>`

</div>
